From 5d1cccbd016d8fec9f53ca6a82f6405fee1f3330 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Skippy contributors <ci@skippy.local>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 02:34:42 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 52/52] skippy: keep resident recurrent checkpoints in shared
 recurrent cells

A resident prefix save copied the lane's recurrent state, 50 MiB for
qwen35-4b, into pinned host memory, and every restore copied it back. In
a GB10 rate-run trace, 104 saves took a median 1.5 ms, and the 16 that
grew the pinned pool took about 20 ms each, all under the runtime lock.

Where the recurrent memory has resident cells, a save now shares the
lane's cell with the cache sequence and a restore shares it with the
restoring lane, so neither copies the state. The lane's next decode
moves it to a free cell through the gather every decode runs anyway, and
the checkpoint's cell is never written. Exporting a checkpoint in a cell
writes that cell, so cold offload and handoff read the same bytes.

Each checkpoint in a cell holds one of the n_rs_resident cells, which
always leaves a lane a free cell to move to. With none free, or with a
rollback pending on the lane, the state is copied to host memory as
before. A cell counts toward the resident byte budget as the copy it
replaces, so eviction is unchanged. A lane that shares a cell cannot
roll back until a decode has moved it to a cell of its own.

Integrated GPUs get four cells per lane by default, since their device
memory is host memory; each costs a recurrent state and its snapshot
rows. Other devices, Metal included, get none, as before.
SKIPPY_RESIDENT_CELLS overrides the count.

skippy_sampling_history_{qwen35,granitehybrid}_resident_cells run the
resident ownership test with one cell: its checkpoint must be in the
cell, and a second checkpoint a host copy.
